```kotlin
class ProfileFragment : Fragment() {
    override fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope.launch {
            viewLifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {
                viewModel.uiState.collect { state ->
                    render(state)   // only runs while the view is at least STARTED
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

`repeatOnLifecycle` automatically restarts the enclosed block every time the lifecycle re-enters the target state (`STARTED`) and cancels it when the lifecycle drops below it — so collection pauses cleanly when the screen is stopped (backgrounded) and resumes cleanly when it's started again, rather than either leaking a collector that keeps running against a stopped screen, or requiring the collector to be manually restarted by hand.

The `viewLifecycleOwner` (not the Fragment's own lifecycle) matters specifically for Fragments — its lifecycle tracks the *view's* lifecycle, which is shorter-lived than the Fragment object's own lifecycle when the Fragment goes onto the back stack, exactly the split-lifecycle distinction from the Activity/Fragment lifecycle topic.
